INT. LIGHTHOUSE LAMP ROOM - NIGHT (1887)

A brass and glass chamber at the top of a stone tower, the great lens turning slowly above. Wind hums in the lantern panes.

SILAS PENHALLOW, fifties, the head keeper, stands at the logbook with a pen. THADDEUS MARROW, twenties, his assistant, kneels at the lens with an oilcan. HOBART VANE, forties, a Trinity House inspector in a wet greatcoat, stands in the doorway with his hat in his hands.

SILAS
Mr. Vane, you've climbed a hundred and forty steps in a gale to tell me my lamp is dim.

HOBART
I climbed them to see whether it was.

THADDEUS
It's not dim, sir. Silas trims the wick every hour.

SILAS
Thaddeus, you needn't defend me.

HOBART
He needn't, but he does, Mr. Marrow. That's a good habit in a young man.

Thaddeus wipes the oilcan on his sleeve and does not look up. Silas closes the logbook.

SILAS
Hobart, you'll take tea before you go down.

HOBART
I'll take it standing.

Silas and Hobart face each other. Neither moves toward the kettle. Thaddeus stands, crosses to it, and lights the spirit stove. Behind him the lens turns, and the beam sweeps the three of them with white light, and goes out over the black water.
